# Solution — GIAM 2.4 Problem 1

## Problem

Write two-column proofs that verify each of the following logical equivalences.

1. $ A\lor(A\land B) \cong A\land(A\lor B) $
2. $ (A\land\lnot B)\lor A \cong A $
3. $ A\lor B \cong A\lor(\lnot A\land B) $
4. $ \lnot(A\lor\lnot B)\lor(\lnot A\land\lnot B) \cong \lnot A $
5. $ A \cong A\land\big((A\lor\lnot B)\lor(A\lor B)\big) $
6. $ (A\land\lnot B)\land(\lnot A\lor B) \cong c $
7. $ A \cong A\land\big(A\lor(A\land(B\lor C))\big) $
8. $ \lnot(A\land B)\land\lnot(A\land C) \cong \lnot A\lor(\lnot B\land\lnot C) $

Throughout, $ t $ denotes a tautology and $ c $ a contradiction.

## The Toolkit

!(96%)[Reference card of the logical laws, and what each of the eight equivalences collapses to](problem-1-laws.svg "A reference card of laws in three groups. Rearranging: commutative, associative, distributive in both directions, idempotent, and double negation. Collapsing: absorption in both forms, complement giving A or not-A equals t and A and not-A equals c, and identity giving A and t equals A and A or c equals A. Moving negations: De Morgan in both forms, and domination giving A or t equals t and A and c equals c. A summary panel lists what each of the eight equivalences collapses to: 1 both sides equal A; 2 equals A; 3 both sides equal A or B; 4 equals not-A; 5 equals A; 6 equals c, a contradiction; 7 equals A; 8 both sides equal not-A or (not-B and not-C). A closing note gives the strategy: start from the messier side and simplify until you reach the other, justifying every line by exactly one named law applied to the line above.")

## The Eight Proofs

### 1. $ A\lor(A\land B) \cong A\land(A\lor B) $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ A\lor(A\land B) $ | given |
| $ \cong A $ | absorption |
| $ \cong A\land(A\lor B) $ | absorption (read right-to-left) |

Both sides collapse to $ A $, so they are equal to each other.

### 2. $ (A\land\lnot B)\lor A \cong A $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ (A\land\lnot B)\lor A $ | given |
| $ \cong A\lor(A\land\lnot B) $ | commutative ($ \lor $) |
| $ \cong A $ | absorption (with $ \lnot B $ in the role of $ B $) |

### 3. $ A\lor B \cong A\lor(\lnot A\land B) $

Starting from the right-hand side, which is the messier one:

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ A\lor(\lnot A\land B) $ | given |
| $ \cong (A\lor\lnot A)\land(A\lor B) $ | distributive ($ \lor $ over $ \land $) |
| $ \cong t\land(A\lor B) $ | complement |
| $ \cong A\lor B $ | identity |

### 4. $ \lnot(A\lor\lnot B)\lor(\lnot A\land\lnot B) \cong \lnot A $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ \lnot(A\lor\lnot B)\lor(\lnot A\land\lnot B) $ | given |
| $ \cong (\lnot A\land\lnot\lnot B)\lor(\lnot A\land\lnot B) $ | De Morgan |
| $ \cong (\lnot A\land B)\lor(\lnot A\land\lnot B) $ | double negation |
| $ \cong \lnot A\land(B\lor\lnot B) $ | distributive (factoring out $ \lnot A $) |
| $ \cong \lnot A\land t $ | complement |
| $ \cong \lnot A $ | identity |

### 5. $ A \cong A\land\big((A\lor\lnot B)\lor(A\lor B)\big) $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ A\land\big((A\lor\lnot B)\lor(A\lor B)\big) $ | given |
| $ \cong A\land\big(A\lor\lnot B\lor A\lor B\big) $ | associative ($ \lor $) |
| $ \cong A\land\big(A\lor A\lor(\lnot B\lor B)\big) $ | commutative, associative |
| $ \cong A\land\big(A\lor(\lnot B\lor B)\big) $ | idempotent |
| $ \cong A\land(A\lor t) $ | complement |
| $ \cong A\land t $ | domination |
| $ \cong A $ | identity |

### 6. $ (A\land\lnot B)\land(\lnot A\lor B) \cong c $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ (A\land\lnot B)\land(\lnot A\lor B) $ | given |
| $ \cong (A\land\lnot B)\land(\lnot A\lor\lnot\lnot B) $ | double negation |
| $ \cong (A\land\lnot B)\land\lnot(A\land\lnot B) $ | De Morgan (read right-to-left) |
| $ \cong c $ | complement |

### 7. $ A \cong A\land\big(A\lor(A\land(B\lor C))\big) $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ A\land\big(A\lor(A\land(B\lor C))\big) $ | given |
| $ \cong A\land A $ | absorption (inner, with $ B\lor C $ in the role of $ B $) |
| $ \cong A $ | idempotent |

### 8. $ \lnot(A\land B)\land\lnot(A\land C) \cong \lnot A\lor(\lnot B\land\lnot C) $

| statement | reason |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ \lnot(A\land B)\land\lnot(A\land C) $ | given |
| $ \cong (\lnot A\lor\lnot B)\land(\lnot A\lor\lnot C) $ | De Morgan (twice) |
| $ \cong \lnot A\lor(\lnot B\land\lnot C) $ | distributive ($ \lor $ over $ \land $, factoring out $ \lnot A $) |

## Remark — The Two Proof Shapes

The eight proofs use only two overall shapes, and recognising which applies saves a great deal of flailing.

**Shape 1 — one side is already simple.** In 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8, one side is a bare letter (or $ c $, or a short expression), so the proof is a straight-line simplification of the *other* side down to it. Always start from the messier expression: simplifying is easier than complicating, because at each step you can see what to cancel.

**Shape 2 — both sides are messy.** In 1 and 3 neither side is obviously simpler. The technique is to reduce **each side separately** to a common **normal form** and let transitivity of $ \cong $ close the gap. In proof 1 both sides collapse to $ A $; the middle line of the two-column table is that shared form, and the second half of the proof is the first half's reasoning run backwards. This is the habit noted in Problem 2.3.2.

## Remark — Proof 6: Spotting a Disguised Complement

Proof 6 is the cleverest of the eight, and the trick is worth isolating. The expression is

$$$
\underbrace{(A\land\lnot B)}_{X}\;\land\;(\lnot A\lor B),
$$$

and the second factor is exactly $ \lnot X $ in disguise: by De Morgan, $ \lnot(A\land\lnot B) \cong \lnot A\lor\lnot\lnot B \cong \lnot A\lor B $. So the whole thing is $ X\land\lnot X $, which the complement law sends to $ c $ immediately. Without noticing the disguise one is tempted to distribute everything out into four terms and cancel them individually — which works, but takes four times as long. **Looking for a sub-expression and its negation** is a habit worth building; it is also what made Problem 2.2.7 short.

## Remark — Absorption Does Most of the Work

Six of the eight proofs (1, 2, 5, 7, and implicitly others) turn on **absorption**, $ A\lor(A\land B)\cong A $ and $ A\land(A\lor B)\cong A $. Proofs 5 and 7 in particular are near-instant once you see the shape $ A\land(A\lor\text{anything}) $: whatever fills the "anything" slot is irrelevant, so the whole expression is just $ A $. In proof 5 the "anything" is $ \lnot B\lor(A\lor B) $; in proof 7 it is $ A\land(B\lor C) $. Both proofs could be done in a single line by citing absorption directly — the longer versions above are given to show the mechanism, but recognising the shape at a glance is the real skill.

## Remark — Why Two-Column Proofs Are Worth the Trouble

A truth table would verify all eight of these mechanically (Problem 2.3.2 did exactly that for a similar list). So why bother with the laws? Three reasons. First, **scale**: proofs 7 and 8 have three variables and so need $ 2^3 = 8 $ rows; a formula with ten variables needs $ 1024 $, and the algebraic route does not grow that way. Second, **discovery**: a truth table can *confirm* an equivalence you already suspect, but it cannot *produce* a simpler form — the laws can, which is what a compiler or circuit optimiser actually needs. Third, **generality**: the two-column format forces every step to name its licence, so the same discipline transfers directly to the theorem-proving of Chapter 3, where no truth table is available at all.

## Remark — On the Notation $ t $ and $ c $

The symbols $ t $ and $ c $ stand for a **tautology** (always true) and a **contradiction** (always false), and they behave like the constants $ 1 $ and $ 0 $ of ordinary algebra. The parallel is exact enough to be a useful memory aid:

| logic | arithmetic analogue |
| :--- | :--- |
| $ A\land t\cong A $ | $ x\cdot 1 = x $ |
| $ A\lor c\cong A $ | $ x + 0 = x $ |
| $ A\land c\cong c $ | $ x\cdot 0 = 0 $ |
| $ A\lor t\cong t $ | *(no analogue)* |

The last row is where the analogy breaks: nothing in arithmetic swallows everything the way $ t $ does under $ \lor $. That extra collapsing power is what makes proofs like 5 possible, and it is the same asymmetry noted in Problem 2.3.1 — Boolean algebra is not arithmetic with new symbols.
